<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Reflux disease with erosive esophagitis (K21.00)</content>. The CT coronary angiogram shows a coronary calcium score of 0 and no plaque or stenosis (CAD-RADS 0), so coronary disease is excluded as the cause of his chest pain. Endoscopy shows Los Angeles grade B esophagitis (mucosal breaks longer than 5 mm, not continuous between folds) above a 2 cm sliding hiatal hernia, with a normal stomach and duodenum and no ulcer. This explains the burning chest and epigastric pain after late meals and the night waking. Peptic ulcer is excluded by the endoscopy, and functional dyspepsia is not diagnosed because a structural cause has been found. Dr. Brandt-Oyelaran and I agree the plan. [S]<list><item>Omeprazole 20 mg 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast for eight weeks, prescribed today and started with tomorrow&apos;s breakfast; stop the calcium carbonate.</item><item>Main meal at least three hours before lying down; head of the bed raised; little or no wine at night.</item><item>No repeat endoscopy is needed for grade B esophagitis.</item><item>Review in four to five weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Epigastric and lower chest pain, now diagnosed (R10.13)</content>. Resolved into the diagnosis of reflux esophagitis, with coronary disease excluded. [S]<list><item>Call 911 for chest pain at rest lasting more than a few minutes, or with breathlessness, sweating or fainting, as before.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opium-containing remedy (F11.90)</content>. Unchanged pattern. Opioids slow stomach emptying and can disturb the esophagus&apos;s muscle, which may add to his symptoms, but the esophagitis needs treatment in its own right. He wants to see how he does once the reflux is treated before deciding anything about the paste. No thoughts of self-harm today. [S]<list><item>No change unless he chooses one.</item><item>Naloxone at the bedside; no alcohol or sleep medicines with the remedy.</item><item>Review at every visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic insomnia (G47.00)</content>. The first CBT-I session was not available this week; part of his night waking is reflux, which should improve on omeprazole. [S]<list><item>First CBT-I session booked in two weeks; a sleep diary and the stimulus-control instructions given today.</item><item>No hypnotic or sedating antihistamine.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Coronary assessment: CT coronary angiography with calcium score</td><td>Once, for stable chest pain with a low-to-intermediate chance of coronary disease</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Calcium score 0; no plaque or stenosis (CAD-RADS 0)</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Upper endoscopy</td><td>Once, for chest pain without classic heartburn after cardiac evaluation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Los Angeles grade B esophagitis; 2 cm sliding hiatal hernia; normal stomach and duodenum</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Helicobacter pylori: gastric biopsies with a rapid urease test</td><td>At endoscopy for dyspepsia, before acid suppression</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Rapid urease test negative; histology sent</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Reflux symptoms: heartburn, chest pain after meals, regurgitation, night waking</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Burning after the evening meal; night waking twice in a week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Alarm features: difficulty or pain swallowing, bleeding, vomiting, weight loss, anemia</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None new; weight steady at 63.2 kg after the 3 kg loss before intake, explained by poor appetite with pain</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Proton pump inhibitor: dose, timing before breakfast, and adherence</td><td>Eight weeks for erosive esophagitis, then the lowest effective dose or on demand</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Omeprazole 20 mg before breakfast for eight weeks, prescribed today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Meal timing, head-of-bed elevation, alcohol at night</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Earlier main meal, head of bed raised, little wine at night</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Opium-containing remedy: amount, pattern, benefits and harms, his own goals</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Most nights as before; no change chosen yet</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home and no alcohol or sedatives with the remedy</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naloxone collected and at the bedside; no wine on remedy nights</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk and safety review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of self-harm; 988 reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Constipation on the opioid</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Polyethylene glycol twice; stools softer</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (six sessions) with sleep diary</td><td>Weekly or every two weeks for six sessions, from intake</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Missed: the first session was due this week but no appointment was available; booked in two weeks, with a sleep diary and stimulus-control instructions meanwhile. No harm judged likely, since part of the night waking is reflux being treated now. Corrective action: the sleep clinician holds a weekly slot for him</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Gastroenterology consultation and endoscopy</td><td>Within one week of intake</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Brandt-Oyelaran this morning</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>A cardiac cause of the